## -----------------------------------------------------------------
## Produkt (Product) Service Sub-Profile
## -----------------------------------------------------------------
## ROLE: Describes the NeTEx touchpoints of Entur's fare-product
##       catalogue (FareProducts / tariffs / fare tables). Grounded in
##       the EFIP-style XML export examples: single tickets, carnets
##       (klippekort), zone- and distance-based fares. Pins the master-
##       and route-data references a product delivery carries, and the
##       fare objects it is built from.
##
## STATUS — DEMONSTRATOR, NOT YET A PRODUCED PROFILE:
##       There is no real product export/import in production yet, so
##       products are NOT part of the Nordic Profile. This sub-profile
##       is an analysis of how a future export could look and which
##       route-data dependencies it implies. It is deliberately loose:
##       it grounds constraints only in classes that exist in the
##       generated NeTEx base and flags everything the EFIP examples
##       need beyond that as PROPOSAL.
##
## GPA PLACEMENT — TWO EXPORT VARIANTS (see examples):
##       (A) EFIP-style: each GenericParameterAssignment is wrapped in
##           its own FareStructureElement and referenced from the
##           ValidableElement via FareStructureElementRef.
##       (B) Current solution: GenericParameterAssignments sit inline
##           under validityParameterAssignments on the FSE / product /
##           ValidableElement.
##       Both express the same constraints — the difference is only the
##       nesting of the GPA. Neither shape below assumes a placement.
##
## Peer of the other service sub-profiles: imports netex-entur.ttl,
## never another sub-profile. The income model builds on products.
##
## Imports: netex-entur.ttl (Entur governance + codespaces)
##          → netex-nordic.ttl → generated NeTEx base (netex:) + nordic vocab
##
## Generated: 2026-08-12
## -----------------------------------------------------------------

@prefix netex:   <https://netex-cen.eu/ontology#> .
@prefix profile: <https://netex-cen.eu/profile#> .
@prefix entur:   <https://entur.org/ontology#> .
@prefix produkt: <https://entur.org/service/produkt#> .
@prefix sh:      <http://www.w3.org/ns/shacl#> .
@prefix rdfs:    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix owl:     <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#> .
@prefix skos:    <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#> .

## --- Import Entur governance ---
<https://entur.org/service/produkt> a owl:Ontology ;
    owl:imports <https://entur.org/ontology> .

## =================================================================
## SERVICE DEFINITION
## =================================================================

produkt:ProductService a entur:SubProfile ;
    rdfs:label "Produkt — Product Service Profile" ;
    skos:definition "Sub-profile for the fare-product catalogue (fare products, tariffs, fare tables). Grounded in the EFIP-style export examples; demonstrator only — no production I/O yet." ;
    entur:basedOn profile:NP .

## =================================================================
## CONSUMED REFERENCES (master + route data a product points at)
## =================================================================
## Every example anchors its fare product to organisation and zone/
## route data by @ref. These are the dependencies to route/master data
## the request asks us to make explicit. Property IRIs follow the base's
## shared lowerCamelCase reference style (as in the other sub-profiles);
## the owning class is carried by the generated restrictions.
##
## NOTE: examples contain SYNTHETIC ids (e.g. NOR:FareZone:*,
## INN:Authority:INN_ID) that need not resolve in Entur's databases —
## so reference targets below are documented, not hard-required.
## -----------------------------------------------------------------

produkt:ProductService produkt:consumes
    ## --- Organisation (ResourceFrame / central registry) ---
    netex:authorityRef ,
    netex:operatorRef ,
    ## --- Fare geography → stop/route data ---
    netex:fareZoneRef ,
    netex:tariffZoneRef ,
    netex:scheduledStopPointRef ,
    ## --- Service scope ---
    netex:lineRef ,
    netex:transportSubmode .

## =================================================================
## OBJECTS IN SCOPE
## =================================================================
## Only fare classes that EXIST in the generated NeTEx base, plus the
## master/route classes the products reference. The richer EFIP fare-
## structure objects are not in the base yet — see PROPOSAL below.
## -----------------------------------------------------------------

produkt:ProductService produkt:requiresClass
    ## --- Fare objects present in the base ---
    netex:FareProduct ,
    netex:FareZone ,
    netex:TariffZone ,
    ## --- Referenced master / route data ---
    netex:Authority ,
    netex:Operator ,
    netex:ScheduledStopPoint ,
    netex:Line .

## =================================================================
## SERVICE-SPECIFIC CONSTRAINTS — SHACL SHAPES
## =================================================================
## Shape naming: produkt:P_{ClassName}Shape
## Grounded only on classes/properties that exist in the base. Both GPA
## placement variants (A/B, see header) satisfy these — the shapes do
## not look inside the GPA nesting.
## -----------------------------------------------------------------

## A fare product is always tied to the organisation selling it.
## Every example carries exactly one OperatorRef on the product.
produkt:P_FareProductShape a sh:NodeShape ;
    sh:targetClass netex:FareProduct ;
    rdfs:label "FareProduct — Product service constraints" ;
    sh:property [
        sh:path netex:operatorRef ;
        sh:minCount 1 ; sh:maxCount 1 ;
        sh:class netex:Operator ;
        sh:name "OperatorRef" ;
        sh:description "The organisation the product is sold under (OperatorRef/AuthorityRef in the examples)"
    ] .

## The fare geography → route-data link: a FareZone groups the
## ScheduledStopPoints that make up the zone. This is the dependency
## that ties a product to the stop-place/route model.
produkt:P_FareZoneShape a sh:NodeShape ;
    sh:targetClass netex:FareZone ;
    rdfs:label "FareZone — Product service constraints" ;
    sh:property [
        sh:path netex:FareZone_Name ;
        sh:minCount 1 ;
        sh:name "Name" ;
        sh:description "Human-readable zone name"
    ] ;
    sh:property [
        sh:path netex:FareZone_ScheduledStopPointRef ;
        sh:class netex:ScheduledStopPoint ;
        sh:name "ScheduledStopPointRef" ;
        sh:description "Stops constituting the zone — the tie to route/stop data"
    ] .

## =================================================================
## PROPOSAL — EFIP fare structure not yet in the generated base
## =================================================================
## The export examples are built from a rich fare-structure vocabulary
## that the generated NeTEx base does NOT define yet. Ungrounded netex:
## IRIs are avoided on purpose; add these to the base first, then move
## them up into requiresClass / shapes here.
##
## SOURCE: cardinalities / enums / class hierarchies below are taken from
## Entur's Nordic NeTEx Profile "fares" (Handbook N801), v0.96 — still
## "awaiting review approval and inclusion in the Nordic NeTEx Profile"
## (last changed 2022-07-28). So it is NOT ratified NP yet: capture as
## PROPOSAL, do not hardcode. Validated example XML for each class lives
## in entur/profile-examples → netex/fares-sales.
##
## PROPOSAL: add fare-product classes to the generated base (hierarchy
##   from the v0.96 profile; all derive from PriceableObject unless noted):
##   - PreassignedFareProduct  < FareProduct < ServiceAccessRight
##   - AmountOfPriceUnitProduct < FareProduct           (carnet/klippekort)
##   - FareStructureElement     < PriceableObject         (carries GPAs)
##   - GenericParameterAssignment < Assignment            (validity params)
##   - ValidableElement         < PriceableObject         (bundles FSEs)
##   - AccessRightInProduct     < VersionedChild          (→ ValidableElementRef)
##   - FareTable / Cell         < PriceableObject / VersionedChild (pricing grid)
##   - FareStructureElementPrice, CellPrice               (Pricing group)
##   - TypeOfPricingRule                                  (VAT/tax category)
##
## PROPOSAL: geographical fare factors (Fare components group):
##   - GeographicalInterval < FareStructureFactor < PriceableObject
##       StartGeographicalValue  xsd:decimal            0:1
##       EndGeographicalValue    xsd:decimal            0:1
##       NumberOfUnits           xsd:integer            0:1
##       IntervalType            IntervalTypeEnum       0:1
##         enum = coupon | distance | section | stop | tariffZone | tariffUnit
##         (the examples use tariffZone and distance)
##   - TimeInterval:  Duration xsd:duration 1:1 (rest 0:1)
##   - FareQuotaFactor: NumberOfUnits xsd:integer 1:1   (klippekort quota)
##
## PROPOSAL: add usage-parameter classes seen in the limitations
##   (all UsageParameter < ValidityParameter):
##   UserProfile, CompanionProfile, Refunding, PurchaseWindow,
##   UsageValidityPeriod, FrequencyOfUse, Interchanging, Reserving,
##   LuggageAllowance, RoundTrip.
##
## PROPOSAL: model the two GPA placements (A EFIP-FSE-wrapped /
##   B current-inline) explicitly once GenericParameterAssignment and
##   FareStructureElement exist, so a single set of shapes can validate
##   both exports. Both must resolve to the same logical constraints.
##
## PROPOSAL: route-data dependencies to formalise once a real export
##   exists (candidates for stricter cross-references / Nordic profiling).
##   Cardinalities from the v0.96 profile where known:
##     - StartTimeAtStopPoint → ScheduledStopPointRef   1:1  (hard tie)
##     - FareZone.members → ScheduledStopPoint   (fare geography ↔ stops)
##     - StopPlace → TariffZoneRef                (already in base)
##     - GeographicalInterval[IntervalType=tariffZone] → FareZone
##     - AuthorityRef / OperatorRef → Organisation (central registry)
##     - product/ValidableElement → TransportSubmode / Line (service scope)
##   Because today's ids are partly synthetic, tighten these to hard
##   sh:class/existence checks only after products are actually
##   produced against resolvable registry data.

## =================================================================
## END OF PRODUCT SERVICE PROFILE
## =================================================================
